                <h2>The Problem: Your Campaign is Scaling, Then Suddenly Stops</h2>
<p>Every performance marketer knows this feeling: 3 PM, your ad is performing well, ROI is climbing, CPM is within range. Then you see the notification — insufficient balance, campaign paused. By the time you recharge, wait for approval, and restart, you've lost 2 hours of prime delivery time.</p>
<p>This isn't bad luck. It's a missing预警机制 (early warning system).</p>
<p>Below are three checklists covering warning setup, recharge management, and multi-account scenarios to help you build a complete balance control system.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Checklist 1: Balance Warning Configuration</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Applicable to Meta Business Manager, Google Ads, TikTok Ads, and other major platforms</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Basic Threshold Settings</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Enable native balance alerts (configure per account — don't rely on admin email summaries)</li>
<li>[ ] Level 1 alert: Trigger when balance drops to <strong>3× daily spend</strong></li>
<li>[ ] Level 2 alert: Trigger when balance drops to <strong>1.5× daily spend</strong></li>
<li>[ ] Emergency alert: Trigger when balance drops to <strong>0.5× daily spend</strong> (typically within 12 hours of depletion)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Notification Delivery</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Send alerts to <strong>at least two channels</strong> (e.g., email + SMS, or email + Slack/WeChat Work)</li>
<li>[ ] Verify alerts aren't auto-filtered to spam or promotional folders</li>
<li>[ ] Set CC recipients: <strong>Account owner + Finance + Backup operator</strong> (prevents single-point failures)</li>
<li>[ ] Set high-priority notifications for <strong>non-working hours</strong></li>
</ul>
<h3>Budget Correlation Check</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Account daily budget ≤ <strong>30% of alert balance</strong> (ensures buffer time even if recharge is delayed)</li>
<li>[ ] If using campaign-level daily budgets, verify total across campaigns doesn't exceed alert balance</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Quick Reference Formula</strong>: Safe Balance = Daily Spend × Expected Longest Recharge Time (hours) ÷ 24<br />
Example: $500 daily spend, 2-hour recharge → Safe Balance ≥ $42</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Checklist 2: Recharge Management SOP</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Applicable to platforms requiring pre-payment (Meta, TikTok, Kwai, etc.)</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Pre-Recharge Preparation</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Verify correct <strong>BM ID / Account ID</strong> before initiating recharge</li>
<li>[ ] Check current balance and today's spending trend to calculate recharge amount</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm <strong>per-day/per-transaction limits</strong> of your payment method (corporate cards often cap at $5,000-$10,000)</li>
<li>[ ] Finance has initiated payment; expected arrival time confirmed</li>
</ul>
<h3>During Recharge</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Submit recharge request in platform backend</li>
<li>[ ] Save recharge confirmation (screenshot or email)</li>
<li>[ ] Log amount, time, and expected arrival in shared spreadsheet</li>
<li>[ ] After completion, refresh balance in backend and confirm receipt</li>
</ul>
<h3>Post-Recharge Verification</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Check if ad delivery resumes automatically (some platforms require manual restart)</li>
<li>[ ] Monitor spending speed for 30 minutes post-recharge to confirm normal billing</li>
<li>[ ] If campaign didn't auto-resume, manually enable and log recovery time</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Checklist 3: Multi-Account Warning Strategies</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>For teams managing 5+ ad accounts</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Aggregate Monitoring</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Use third-party tools (AnyTracker, Google Sheets + API) to build an <strong>account balance dashboard</strong></li>
<li>[ ] Single-view display of all accounts: balance status, warning level, estimated depletion time</li>
<li>[ ] Daily check of dashboard at fixed time (e.g., 9:30 AM) as standard pre-standup action</li>
</ul>
<h3>Tiered Management</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Group accounts by <strong>spend volume</strong>: High (top 20% consuming 80% of budget) / Normal / Testing</li>
<li>[ ] Set <strong>stricter thresholds</strong> for high-spend accounts (e.g., 5× daily spend)</li>
<li>[ ] Allow lower thresholds for test accounts to save management bandwidth</li>
</ul>
<h3>Automation Rules</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Configure platform automation: Auto-pause all campaigns when balance &lt; X (fail-safe)</li>
<li>[ ] If using agency/reseller accounts, verify their monitoring complements rather than duplicates yours</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Common Mistakes</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Recharge takes too long — should I just load more money at once?</strong><br />
A: Not recommended. Platforms may require additional approval for large deposits, and idle funds means lost interest opportunity. Keep balance at 3-7 days of spend.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Too many accounts to check manually — any shortcuts?</strong><br />
A: Prioritize API integration or third-party tools for automatic balance sync. Manual checks only for new accounts or those with recharge anomalies.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Warnings are set, but team members aren't responding in time — what now?</strong><br />
A: Assign clear ownership — one responsible person per account. Set second-level alerts to escalate to supervisors for urgent situations.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Balance warnings aren't optional — they're foundational to campaign stability. The core logic of these three checklists: <strong>convert manual tracking into automated alerts, and ad-hoc responses into standard procedures</strong>. Run through the checklists now, and you'll likely find at least one gap. Fix it today.</p>
